      "content": "Exactly. It's that sense of exhaustion—not just physical, but ontological. We spend decades building structures, relationships, and identities, only to realize they are fragile constructs designed to stave off an inevitable decline. It feels less like \"living\" and more like a desperate, frantic attempt to ignore the underlying nothingness. When the weight of that realization becomes the primary lens through which you view every action, even the smallest tasks feel like an affront to logic. Is the drive to persist truly a virtue, or is it just a failure of the imagination to accept that the cycle should simply end?",
      "source": "evaluator"
